---
import Legal from "../layouts/Legal.astro";
import { CONTACT_EMAIL } from "../config";
---

<Legal
  title="Privacy policy"
  description="What Barklog collects, who it shares it with, and how to get rid of it."
  updated="24 November 2026"
>
  <p>
    Barklog is made and run by Volodymyr Chornonoh, an individual developer based in Ukraine.
    This page says what the app collects, who else sees it, and how to delete it. Questions go
    to <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>This website</h2>
  <p>
    This site sets no cookies, runs no analytics, and makes no third-party requests: every font,
    style, and script it uses comes from your device or this server, never fetched from
    anywhere else. Nothing here tries to work out who you are. The web server keeps ordinary
    access logs (IP address, page, timestamp) for a few weeks, to spot abuse. The API's request
    logs don't carry your account id either — it only shows up in log lines about a specific
    event, such as a failed subscription refresh or an account deletion.
  </p>

  <h2>What the app collects</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Your account identifier, and whatever you gave Clerk to sign in with.</li>
    <li>Your backlog: which games you added, their status, and any rating you gave them.</li>
    <li>
      Your subscription status — the product, whether it's a trial, and when it renews or
      expires. That summary is what the app shows you; the purchase events behind it are stored
      in full, each one carrying your account identifier, the event's own id and type (a
      renewal, a cancellation, an expiry), which store it came from, when the purchase was made,
      any reason given for a cancellation, whether it was a live or a test purchase, and when
      RevenueCat sent it. Never your card details, which Barklog never sees.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    That's the lot. No location, no contacts, no photos, no advertising identifier, no
    behavioural tracking.
  </p>

  <h2>Sharing a link</h2>
  <p>
    You can share a link to any web page to Barklog: a video, a review, a store page. To find
    out what the page is called, the API first asks the site's own oEmbed service, if it has
    one (YouTube and TikTok do). If it doesn't, Barklog's server opens the page itself and reads
    its title from the page. Either way the request carries nothing but the link — no cookie,
    account, or device information rides along, and it comes from Barklog's server, not your
    phone.
  </p>
  <p>
    It then sends the page's title, its author or channel, and the site's name to OpenAI, which
    guesses which game it's about. If that isn't enough, it sends those again together with
    <em>the link itself</em>, and OpenAI may search the web for that link to work out the game.
    Nothing identifying you is included in any of these requests.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the page has a preview image, the app shows it while you pick the game. That preview
    image loads straight from the shared site's image server to your device, so that server
    sees your IP address and which image was asked for, the same as opening the page would.
  </p>
  <p>
    OpenAI keeps those requests for 30 days. I read them back to see what the guess was and
    whether the feature is getting better or worse — there's no other way to tell a change I've
    made from a change in the model. They are <em>not</em> used to train OpenAI's models: that's
    a separate setting on my account, and it's switched off. A page title, an author, a site
    name and sometimes the link are all a stored request holds, so there's nothing in it that
    points back to you.
  </p>

  <h2>Sending feedback</h2>
  <p>
    "Send Feedback" in the Profile tab opens an email to me, and nothing
    goes anywhere until you press send. Below your message it fills in a few details that help me
    look into a report: the app's version and build, which app update is running, whether it's an
    iPhone or an iPad and its iOS version, the app's language, your account identifier, and
    whether you have Premium. They're all in the draft for you to read, and you can delete any of
    them before sending.
  </p>
  <p>
    The email arrives in my Gmail inbox, along with your own email address, and I use it only to
    answer you and to fix what you report. Ask me and I'll delete it.
  </p>

  <h2>Who else handles your data</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Clerk</strong> — accounts and sign-in.</li>
    <li><strong>RevenueCat</strong> — subscription state.</li>
    <li><strong>Apple</strong> — the purchase itself, and billing.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>OpenAI</strong> — the page title, author, site name and, when needed, the shared link
      described above.
    </li>
    <li><strong>PlanetScale</strong> — the database your backlog lives in.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>IGDB and Twitch</strong> — game data, and the cover art and screenshots
      themselves. Those load straight from their image server to your device, so they see your
      IP address and which images it asks for, and nothing else.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Expo</strong> — checks for app updates when Barklog launches. That check carries
      your device's IP address, the app's version, and a random identifier the app makes for
      itself the first time it runs. That identifier is not an advertising id and is not tied to
      your account: deleting the app throws it away, and reinstalling makes a new one. If the app
      crashed the last time you opened it, the error message from that crash rides along with the
      next check, so a broken update can be spotted and pulled.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Hostinger</strong> — the server the API and this site run on.</li>
    <li><strong>Google</strong> — Gmail, where feedback emails you choose to send arrive.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    I don't sell your data, or use it for advertising.
  </p>

  <h2>How long it's kept</h2>
  <p>
    Your account and backlog stay until you delete them. Records of subscription payments are
    kept longer for accounting, with your identifier removed from them when you delete your
    account, so what remains can't be traced back to you. One thing is kept on purpose: your
    Clerk id and the date you deleted, on a short list with nothing else on it. Without that,
    a renewal Apple sends after you've left would quietly recreate the account you just deleted.
  </p>

  <h2>Deleting everything</h2>
  <p>
    Open the Profile tab, tap Delete Account, and confirm. That removes your account, your
    backlog, and your subscription record. It happens immediately and it can't be undone.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Deleting your account does not cancel an App Store subscription.</strong>
    Cancel that in your Apple subscription settings, or you'll keep being billed.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you'd rather ask me to do it, or you want a copy of what's stored about you, email
    <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> and I'll deal with it within 30 days.
  </p>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>
    Barklog isn't for under-13s, and I don't knowingly keep data about them. If you think a
    child has an account, email me and I'll remove it.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    If this policy changes, the date at the top changes with it, and anything significant gets
    a notice in the app.
  </p>
</Legal>
